{{> Top frame}}
<header>
  <p class="kicker">Lesson 6</p>
  <h1>Tests</h1>
  <p class="lede">A page is a value until the server sends it, so a test can compare the
  whole response with <code>==</code>.</p>
</header>

<section class="turn-box">
  <h2>Your turn</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><p>In a second terminal, in this folder:</p>
    <pre class="term"><code>roc test main.roc</code></pre>
    <p>The tests are at the bottom of <code>main.roc</code>, each an <code>expect</code>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Add one for your route from Lesson 5:</p>
    <pre><code>expect page("/greet/Ada") == Ok(Rocstache.html(Greeting.template({ frame: frame("/routes"), name: "Ada" })))</code></pre>
    <p>Run <code>roc test main.roc</code> again. Then change <code>"Ada"</code> on one side only and
    watch it fail, naming the values.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>What a test can check</h2>
  <p>Which template a path chooses, and every field it is given: the response is a record
  holding the template's value, and values compare. A test can also reach inside one:</p>
  <pre><code>expect
	match page("/lists") {
		Ok({ status: 200, body: Html({ template: Lists(lists), .. }), .. }) =&gt; lists.menu.dishes == dishes
		_ =&gt; False
	}</code></pre>
  <p>The HTML itself is roux's to get right, and roux tests its renderer against an oracle
  over thousands of generated templates. Your tests are about your app.</p>
</section>
